# evidence/audit-chain.jsonl is a strict append-only event log. Its `_meta`
# row declares the append-only contract, and the codex review bot enforces
# "no deleted rows" on every PR that touches it — the policy is verified
# both by contract and by review.
#
# With 14+ parallel merge-variant PRs each appending one row, default merge
# semantics produced a continuous merge-storm: every advance of `dev`
# re-conflicted ~13 other branches against the trailing-line region. Git's
# built-in `union` driver concatenates both sides of an appended-only hunk
# instead of marking conflict, which is the correct semantic here.
#
# Trade-off acknowledged: if a future change ever DOES need to legitimately
# delete a row (e.g. a hard-redact for compliance), `merge=union` will
# silently keep the deleted row when re-merging across branches. That
# scenario must be done as a single linearised commit on `dev`, not in a
# parallel PR.
#
# Scope is deliberately limited to this one file. `registry/fixuptasks.jsonl`
# was considered but excluded: its history shows in-place row REPLACEMENTS
# (status flips on existing rows) so `union` would silently keep stale +
# updated variants of the same logical record, exactly the failure mode the
# codex reviewer flagged on the initial PR.
evidence/audit-chain.jsonl merge=union

# Cargo.lock is generated, but it is not append-only: package sections can be added, removed,
# or version-replaced by independent branches. Git's text merge and `merge=union` can silently
# keep stale package entries, so Cargo.lock uses the structural three-way driver in
# tools/oya-cargo-lock-merge-driver-app. The driver merges disjoint [[package]] additions and
# exits 1 on same-package version divergence or removal-vs-edit conflicts.
Cargo.lock merge=cargo-lock
